b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172910 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172910 #edb/ref 1061 32015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04179788-29f9-5a3f-b42b-49e85f081fd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172911 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172911 #edb/ref 1061 32016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172911 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8350a9fc-df2c-5b2f-8b39-c0eb94143560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172911 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173032 #edb/ref 1061 39011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67175d9a-d110-565e-888e-e308d3e1b77e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173033 #edb/ref 1061 39013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f02ee5e-0eca-5a7e-8131-52282b1ee808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173034 #edb/ref 1061 39014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6e3bd9e-f671-5ff3-9479-7d06fa3c1f75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173220 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that if a random variable $X$ follows a *discrete* uniform distribution, then it has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/0a/0abfcf5eccaaaf22dbc144f58098d394297a5819a1857ff316ce9e9d01e44f7d.svg)\n\nLet's now suppose that \n\n$$\nX\\sim U\\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are integers. In other words, we're concerned with the special case where the support $S$ of $X$ contains every integer between $a$ and $b$ inclusive. \n\nIn this case, it can be shown that the mean value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173220 #edb/ref 1065 6456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173220 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8f4d7b80-6d8f-5618-b9a4-a8041941041e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173220 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173221 #edb/ref 1064 "Here, we will show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/36/36e73322640df22386428fdc41d1940ef7469587566c2f4f24936158292c087c.svg)\n\nBy the definition of expected value for discrete random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}xf(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the term in the square brackets is an arithmetic series with the first term $a$ and the last term $b.$ Therefore, using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{|S|}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{|S|}}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173221 #edb/ref 1065 6458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173221 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee8590ba-00d0-5dce-a5c6-f69f23dd1dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173221 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving the Formula for the Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173222 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, |S|\\}.\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}f(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot {\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot |S|\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\cancel{|S|}\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+|S|}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-{(\\text{E}[X])}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-{(\\frac{|S|+1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-\\frac{(|S|+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{12}-\\frac{3(|S|+1)^{2}}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(2(2|S|+1)-3(|S|+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(4|S|+2-3|S|-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(|S|-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(|S|-1)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173222 #edb/ref 1065 6459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173222 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3ac5d5b0-adb4-5096-93dc-1a400ec42c5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759