5605-a6a4-5e9539c29dc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523926 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the daily maximum temperature (rounded to the nearest degree) on a randomly selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{25+36}{2}=30.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the maximum temperature is ${30.5}^{∘}C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $70$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $10$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the expected value of the chosen issue?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17dce6e5-6992-5bd8-99ae-27a5145f8ae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523932 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the selected issue. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{11,12…,70\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{11,12,…,70\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11+70}{2}=40.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $40.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1071 "A traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Assuming that each day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September has the same probability of being chosen, what is the standard deviation of the selected days? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e997cfb-3847-52d2-9127-67ab63d15dde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523936 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the date of the selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =14-7+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{63}{12}}≈2.29.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1071 "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $0$ through $9.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the standard deviation of the numbers of the selected marbles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2200f2ed-00f4-5dc2-a53c-43c556e4cd84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523938 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-0+1 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{10}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{99}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{99}{12}}≈2.87.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1071 "Ann chooses a card at random from a deck of cards labeled $28$ through $65.$  What is the expected value of the chosen number?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dac21a9-fd23-5a65-b9b0-0999f6a3ac35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523952 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the value of the number chosen. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{28+65}{2}=46.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $46.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1071 "A spinner divided into eight equal sections numbered $2$ through $9$ is spun. What is the variance of the numbers obtained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56f939dc-3462-5544-b92f-c768cb817339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523955 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number obtained by spinning the spinner. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-2+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}=5.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the numbers obtained is $5.25$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1071 "In a video game, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $80$ and $125,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability of occurring, what is the standard deviation of the number of obstacles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57d50a92-18de-56e3-a7c3-f6f999c0fbca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523957 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =125-80+1 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{46}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2\\,115}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,115}{12}}≈13.28.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1071 "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $55$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $7$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the standard deviation of the issue numbers he could select? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0deae3f-c105-5c17-970a-aab6db3301cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524014 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =55-8+1 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{48}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2303}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2303}{12}}≈13.85.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1071 "In a math test, students have to answer a multiple-choice question, where the answers are labeled from $0$ to $6.$ If Steph selects an answer at random, what is the expected value of her chosen answer?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9910a94-d71d-54e3-8cb5-b52ba8bdfed9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524029 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{0+6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen answer is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1071 "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $-3$ through $8.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the expected value of the chosen marble?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8cca8da-6044-5230-8f59-af0159f2bec9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524039 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-3+8}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen marble is $2.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
